                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_appreciative:  `<strong>Good instinct.</strong> Starting from what already works changes who shows up. Instead of another “where we are failing” slide the council can recite, you are inquiring into the positive core — and that is a deliberate, evidence-based choice, not a slogan.`,
                    coach_r1_hybrid:        `<strong>A hybrid hedges.</strong> Opening with the gap analysis still puts a deficit slide first — the workforce stops listening before the appreciative session begins. If you believe the method failed, lead with the positive core, don't bury it after the deck.`,
                    coach_r1_deficit:       `<strong>Your first move is another slide titled “Where We Are Failing.”</strong> The last three programs opened exactly that way, and two are in the initiative graveyard. The works council can recite this deck. What does starting from what already works change about who shows up?`,
                    coach_r1_repeat:        `<strong>That's how you get the same result.</strong> A new deck, a new dashboard and more urgency is precisely the pattern that died twice. You can't out-push change fatigue — you have to change the method, not the volume.`,
                    coach_r1_strong:        `<strong>Sharp start.</strong> An appreciative frame plus a real map of the positive core — the waste-cutting site and the regenerative farmers — gives you genuine seeds to amplify. That is what makes the restart generative rather than another launch.`,
                    coach_r1_nocore:        `<strong>“Nothing worth amplifying” is the deficit reflex in disguise.</strong> There are pockets where sustainability already works here; if you can't see them, you'll default to cataloguing failure again — and the workforce has rejected that three times.`,
                    coach_r1_lowcandor:     `<strong>If you won't name the real failure, you'll repeat it.</strong> This was a method failure, not a motivation or target failure. Low willingness to say that out loud means you'll relaunch the same top-down push under a friendlier name.`,
                    coach_r2_full:          `<strong>The full 4-D arc is the engine of ownership.</strong> Discovery, Dream, Design and Destiny carry people from stories of what works to co-created commitments with named owners — that is how a dialogue produces forward movement, not just a nice afternoon.`,
                    coach_r2_discovery:     `<strong>Discovery alone gathers the stories — then strands them.</strong> If you collect what gives life and then decide centrally, you've reopened the top-down loop. The Design and Destiny phases are where ownership actually transfers.`,
                    coach_r2_dream:         `<strong>A big visioning event spikes energy and then fades.</strong> Dream without Discovery's grounding and Design's commitments is the inspirational kickoff that the three dead programs also had. Energy is not yet ownership.`,
                    coach_r2_broad:         `<strong>The right people are in the room.</strong> Employees, the works council, site managers and farmer-suppliers co-designing together is how voice becomes ownership — and how the works council moves from withholding cooperation to co-authoring the restart.`,
                    coach_r2_narrow:        `<strong>A narrow room rebuilds the centre.</strong> Managers and the CSO designing it and briefing everyone afterwards is a top-down plan with a participative label. The shop floor and the council will read it as exactly that.`,
                    coach_r2_token:         `<strong>A sign-off committee is not co-design.</strong> Asking a representative group to approve a plan leadership already drafted is textbook tokenism — the fatigued workforce smells it, and trust falls further.`,
                    coach_r2_burst:         `<strong>You've booked all three interventions in ten days.</strong> You're shouting at exhausted people whose change-engagement is 41% and falling. Each over-ask deepens the cynicism. You have a budget of three for the whole 90 days — why spend it like this?`,
                    coach_r2_nointervention:`<strong>No intervention, no dialogue.</strong> You can't transfer ownership by memo. Commit at least one of your three org-wide interventions to convening real voice, or the restart never leaves the slide deck.`,
                    coach_r3_cocreated:     `<strong>Ownership transfers where the work happens.</strong> Letting the people who will execute the initiatives choose and own them is the difference between 30% adoption and durable uptake. This is the whole point of the dialogue.`,
                    coach_r3_topdown:       `<strong>You ran a beautiful Discovery session — and then the CSO picked the list alone.</strong> The shop floor will smell the rebrand instantly. What did the dialogue actually decide, if the answer was predetermined?`,
                    coach_r3_adopted:       `<strong>Adopted impact is the right ranking.</strong> Impact times realistic adoption is what moves the dial — and pairing it with genuine ownership means the initiatives you back will actually be executed, not admired on a dashboard.`,
                    coach_r3_maxpaper:      `<strong>This initiative cuts emissions most on paper — and it's the most disruptive to operators already at their limit.</strong> The last “highest-impact” program got under 30% adoption. Impact times adoption is what moves the dial. What's your realistic adopted impact, not your theoretical one?`,
                    coach_r3_safe:          `<strong>Only the easy initiatives keeps the peace but barely moves the targets.</strong> Low-disruption choices protect capacity, but you still owe the board movement on emissions, waste and plant-based share. Adopted impact, not just comfort.`,
                    coach_r3_nocapacity:    `<strong>You're ignoring the absorptive limit.</strong> Loading an exhausted workforce with change and no protected capacity is how the last “highest-impact” push got under 30% adoption. Respect the limit or the initiatives stall on contact.`,
                    coach_r4_rhythm:        `<strong>A 12-month cadence with positive-core stewards is what sustains generativity.</strong> Energy without rhythm fades in six weeks — keeping the strengths story visible and owned month after month is how this stays alive in month nine.`,
                    coach_r4_oneoff:        `<strong>That kickoff felt great — generativity spiked. So did the launch of the three programs that died.</strong> Energy without rhythm fades in six weeks. How does this stay generative in month nine, and who keeps the positive core visible?`,
                    coach_r4_lagging:       `<strong>Lagging KPIs alone are too slow.</strong> Emissions, waste and plant-based share confirm progress months late — without leading signals of adoption and generativity you won't see the restart slipping until it has already faded.`,
                    coach_r4_proofnone:     `<strong>“The numbers will follow” is what the dead programs said.</strong> A vision narrative with no early evidence is indistinguishable from the last three launches. Bring adoption signals, or the board hears another inspirational deck.`,
                    coach_r4_honest:        `<strong>That's a board-ready, honest answer.</strong> Showing exactly what the dialogue decided, which teams now own each commitment, and a sustaining cadence is a restart that survives — not a number that collapses once attention moves on.`,
                    coach_r4_rebrand:       `<strong>New language, same top-down plan — that's the tokenism they're testing for.</strong> If the targets and plan are firmly “ours” and the dialogue just reworded them, you've rebranded the push that already failed twice. Did ownership really move?`,
                    coach_r4_declare:       `<strong>The kickoff felt extraordinary — so did the three that died.</strong> Declaring victory on launch energy ignores the months when initiatives decay back to baseline. Who owns this in month nine, and how do you even see if it's slipping?`,
                    coach_over:             `<strong>You've exceeded the three-intervention cap.</strong> Over-engaging an exhausted workforce is itself a failure mode — each extra org-wide ask deepens the fatigue you were sent to repair. The limit is the discipline, not an obstacle.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
